      "role": "user",
      "content": "That \"goblin's lair\" analogy doesn't really help me with the billing. I need to stay on the clinical side of this. \n\nRegarding the breakdown you suggested: most of these cases aren't just simple keratosis. The morphology is much more aggressive and visually \"busy.\" We're seeing these irregular, knobby, and almost parasitic-looking clusters that seem to erupt in a very disorganized, jagged fashion. The texture is often described by the clinicians as \"gnarled\" or \"crusty\" in a way that defies the standard L71 series.\n\nIs there a way to capture that specific, chaotic, and hypertrophic nature of the growth within the hierarchy? For example, if the morphology is characterized by these weird, uneven, and clustered protrusions, is there a high-level ICD-10 category for \"disorganized\" or \"atypical\" textural patterns that would satisfy an auditor better than just \"unspecified\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
